WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.780
A $400,000 polymarket insider case now goes to a New York jury. This is Prediction Markets Daily, episode 41. Journalism and education never abet. An indigenous law in Canada, kalshi's New Jersey extension, and how it works on replication markets follow. A special operation soldier is accused of using classified information about the capture of Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro. Bloomberg Law reported that a federal judge denied his motion to dismiss the criminal charges last week.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:34.240
league. Jurors have to decide whether those bets were a hedging derivative called a swap.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.740
The outlet calls it an anticipated first-of-its-kind trial, a test of how far U.S. prosecutors can

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:46.100
push alleged insider trading in this industry. That denial is what puts the case on a trial

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.611
track. Canada's first indigenous prediction markets law has launched.

00:00:51.111 --> 00:00:56.511
SBC News reported today. Calche received a second Supreme Court extension in its New Jersey

00:00:56.511 --> 00:01:01.911
case, gaming America reported. Nasdaq wants to launch inflation prediction markets,

00:01:02.431 --> 00:01:07.151
ats.io reported, and that is an aim not a product users can trade yet.

00:01:08.184 --> 00:01:13.164
A Guardian column out today argues that Kalshi and Polymarket sell the idea of crowd wisdom

00:01:13.164 --> 00:01:18.864
while a few skilled traders set the prices. A Kalshi spokesperson told Reuters the data is

00:01:18.864 --> 00:01:24.464
bottom up, not elites driven, and that these markets show what people say when money is on the line.

00:01:24.464 --> 00:01:30.564
authors of a much-cited 2025 Vanderbilt University study infer from results in

00:01:30.564 --> 00:01:35.504
which Polly Market beat polls an alignment with wisdom of crowd's research. A

00:01:35.504 --> 00:01:39.604
working paper from the London Business School and Yale University built on

00:01:39.604 --> 00:01:44.144
public Polly Market trades found almost every wager or no better than chance.

00:01:44.744 --> 00:01:48.624
Accuracy in that paper came from a minority of persistently skilled traders

00:01:48.624 --> 00:01:53.684
around 3% of accounts while the crowd supplied most of the volume and little of

00:01:53.684 --> 00:01:59.104
the information. The Wall Street Journal, looking at 1.6 million polymarket wallets traded

00:01:59.104 --> 00:02:05.744
since November 2022, found three quarters of the profits in 0.1% of the accounts. Of

00:02:05.744 --> 00:02:11.944
at least 2.3 million accounts, just 2,000 netted almost half a billion dollars. Pew research

00:02:11.944 --> 00:02:17.844
found the median polymarket user placed 46 trades across 10 active trading days in 2026.

00:02:17.844 --> 00:02:23.504
A study funded by the Brennan Center found these prices shift voters' expectations substantially,

00:02:23.944 --> 00:02:30.144
and Barnard Economist Rajeev Sethi told NPR that beliefs about an election can change what happens.

00:02:31.002 --> 00:02:35.082
Kodair Online Chief Executive Aviv Cher told iGaming Business,

00:02:35.642 --> 00:02:38.822
the company is only watching prediction markets and has not entered.

00:02:39.322 --> 00:02:43.202
He calls a move a realistic option and a possible portfolio addition,

00:02:43.202 --> 00:02:47.862
and he said it could fit strategy in Mexico towards Spanish-speaking people in the Southern

00:02:47.862 --> 00:02:48.782
United States.

00:02:49.242 --> 00:02:55.902
Also today, Polly Market returned $3.9 million to traders, Security Week reported, as

00:02:55.902 --> 00:02:58.282
Debug Bear cited a DNS hijack.

00:02:58.742 --> 00:03:03.882
Democrats are questioning whether X is giving Polly Market preferential treatment, Semaphore

00:03:03.882 --> 00:03:04.362
reported.

00:03:04.882 --> 00:03:08.322
This show takes no money from any venue and holds no position.

00:03:08.322 --> 00:03:15.082
Traders on Calche and Polymarket went door-to-door in Maine hunting a mid-term edge, Bloomberg reported.

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:21.682
Users of Calche and Polymarket traded Maryland races despite a ban, RG.org reported.

00:03:22.322 --> 00:03:28.322
Major wagers landed on British Columbia elections as these markets influence grew, CBC reported.

00:03:29.373 --> 00:03:38.373
Democrats sweeping both chambers sits at 71.5% on Polymarket, and that figure is an implied probability, not a forecast from this show.

00:03:38.713 --> 00:03:43.053
A Democratic House is priced at 94.5% on the same venue.

00:03:43.613 --> 00:03:47.093
A Democratic Senate is priced at 72.5% there.

00:03:47.753 --> 00:03:54.253
On a polymarket map of Senate swing states, the leading contract is none of them, at 31.5%,

00:03:54.253 --> 00:03:55.313
up 7 points.

00:03:55.853 --> 00:04:03.333
Republicans winning the Senate is at 21.4% on Manafold, and that venue uses play money, mana, not dollars.

00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:10.153
Whether you can reach these contracts depends on the country, province, or state you live in, and that map is still changing.

00:04:10.813 --> 00:04:18.833
Polymarket posted a new contract on a Democratic sweep and a Chinese AI model ranked number one, both by the end of 2027.

00:04:19.845 --> 00:04:25.185
Two unrelated outcomes sit inside one claim, a combinatorial question rather than a single event.

00:04:25.785 --> 00:04:33.305
A replication market prices whether a published study will hold up when someone runs it again, and that mechanism is how it works in this research.

00:04:33.305 --> 00:04:37.985
Traders buy if they think the finding survives and sell if they think it fails.

00:04:38.525 --> 00:04:43.265
The contract pays on a successful rerun and pays nothing if the rerun breaks the result.

00:04:43.965 --> 00:04:46.205
Nobody in the market has to touch a lab bench.

00:04:46.685 --> 00:04:52.325
A 2015 paper in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences built markets on 44 studies

00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:54.005
from prominent psychology journals.

00:04:54.545 --> 00:04:59.325
Those papers were the ones later rerun in the Reproducibility Project, Psychology.

00:04:59.325 --> 00:05:03.285
The list was fixed, so the questions did not wander with the news.

00:05:03.845 --> 00:05:08.385
Each claim settled on an actual replication, not on whether the original authors still

00:05:08.385 --> 00:05:09.205
liked their paper.

00:05:09.845 --> 00:05:14.725
Those prices predicted the replication outcomes well, and they beat a survey of individual

00:05:14.725 --> 00:05:15.245
forecasts.

00:05:15.965 --> 00:05:19.125
The same paper notes a cost estimate used to explain the hurry.

00:05:20.025 --> 00:05:25.005
Irreproducible pre-clinical research alone has been put at $28 billion a year in the

00:05:25.005 --> 00:05:25.725
United States.

00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:28.325
That dollar figure is not a market profit.

00:05:28.325 --> 00:05:31.665
It is the waste a faster screen is supposed to help avoid.

00:05:32.185 --> 00:05:36.965
The authors say nothing else was in place to flag weak findings quickly, and that markets

00:05:36.965 --> 00:05:42.885
fit that gap. A 2025 paper in the proceedings of the human factors and ergonomic society

00:05:42.885 --> 00:05:48.765
starts from the cracks in the older design. Thin liquidity, slow payouts, and scarce experts

00:05:48.765 --> 00:05:54.245
are the three faults it names. One arrival can shove a thin price. A correct trader may

00:05:54.245 --> 00:05:59.185
weight years to get paid. The people who know the method may never trade. The proposal

00:05:59.185 --> 00:06:05.405
blends three automated market makers with reputation-based staking and staged payouts. A university

00:06:05.405 --> 00:06:10.505
or a grant agency can pick among three built-in pricing rules meant to keep the price meaningful

00:06:10.865 --> 00:06:16.945
when only a few people show up. Reputation can back a position, not only cash, and staged

00:06:16.945 --> 00:06:22.285
payouts paid before the final replication lands. The authors call that a triage tool for

00:06:22.285 --> 00:06:27.445
funders and a way for researchers to gauge a new finding in a market they describe as liquid

00:06:27.445 --> 00:06:34.085
and intellectually rewarding. Those 44 psychology papers are the job the 2015 markets actually did,

00:06:34.645 --> 00:06:39.785
and they beat the survey. The later paper treats thin books and slow pay as the standing weakness,

00:06:40.265 --> 00:06:45.425
and it answers with that hybrid. Read a science price as a probability understated rules,

00:06:45.425 --> 00:06:48.705
settled by a replication, not by the journal's name.

00:06:49.425 --> 00:06:50.745
A survey is a different tool.

00:06:51.445 --> 00:06:53.925
On those 44 studies, the market did better.

00:06:54.365 --> 00:06:56.545
A price with almost no one on the other side

00:06:56.545 --> 00:06:58.585
is the fault the hybrid is built to repair.

00:06:59.325 --> 00:07:01.485
None of this is a suggestion to trade anything.

00:07:02.065 --> 00:07:04.865
It is a reason to ask who can trade when they get paid

00:07:05.225 --> 00:07:06.865
and what event settles the question.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:10.860
Before we go, Polly Markets' balance of power contract

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:12.880
is scheduled to close on November 4.

